# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment and tooling
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.DS_Store
.vscode/
.idea/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Inputs — downloadable from the sources listed in the report's references.
#
# Not merely large: three files here are individually over GitHub's 100 MB hard
# limit (models/bert_sentiment_model/model.safetensors at 418 MB, and the two
# Dataset/Glasses/attention_label CSVs at 313 MB each), so the repository
# cannot carry them at all.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Dataset/
models/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-prediction outputs — regenerated by `python -m xai.run --all`.
#
# 1.5 GB of cards, panels and attribution arrays: 5,561 predictions x up to
# four methods. The aggregate JSON that the report is actually built from is
# tracked (see below), so every number in the report stays checkable without
# cloning the raw outputs.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
artifacts/*/explanations/
artifacts/*/saliency/
artifacts/*/train_embeddings/
artifacts/*.log

# predictions.jsonl is NOT ignored for movie and glasses: it holds every
# attribution behind every figure, so a clone can inspect any explanation and
# recompute the movie behavioural probes. Movie review text is Apache 2.0 and
# redistributable; glasses records carry file paths only, no image data.
#
# It does not make everything re-runnable: xai.faithfulness re-runs inference and
# needs models/, xai.evaluate scores against the human annotation in Dataset/, and
# the glasses shape probe needs the saliency arrays above. Those are checkable
# against the aggregate JSON, which is why that is tracked.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tweet-derived text — excluded on licensing grounds, not size.
#
# The Tweet Sentiment Extraction competition rules state: "You agree not to
# transmit, duplicate, publish, redistribute or otherwise provide or make
# available the Data to any party not participating in the Competition."
#
# These two paths reproduce that corpus in bulk — predictions.jsonl embeds all
# 2,748 test tweets verbatim plus 5,569 training tweets as prototype
# neighbours, and the CSV repeats them. Aggregate statistics derived from the
# corpus are not the corpus, so artifacts/tweet/summary.json and the
# behavioural results stay; the text itself does not.
#
# The report quotes twelve tweets as four worked examples with their retrieved
# neighbours, which is ordinary scholarly quotation rather than redistribution.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
artifacts/tweet/predictions.jsonl

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Glasses saliency images -- excluded on two independent grounds, neither size.
#
# 1. Saliency-Bench states no licence. Public availability is not a grant:
#    copyright attaches on creation, so using the data for research is one
#    thing and redistributing derivatives of it is another. The dataset is
#    linked and credited instead -- see NOTICE and scripts/get_data.py.
#
# 2. They are identifiable photographs of people, which is personal data
#    whatever the copyright position.
#
# The files stay on disk; they are simply not published. No glasses result is
# lost: the per-prediction records, the aggregate statistics and the report all
# remain, and the report's three worked examples stay on the ordinary basis
# that a paper may illustrate its findings.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# These three notebooks' saved cell outputs display 84 tweets between them.
# They stay on disk exactly as written -- nothing is stripped or lost -- they
# simply are not published. The tweet pipeline is still represented in the
# repository by 02_tokenize_and_encode, 03_train_model and shap.ipynb, none of
# which carry corpus text.
Notebooks/Tweet sentiment/prototype_tweet_sentiment.ipynb
Notebooks/Tweet sentiment/04a_lime_explainer.ipynb
Notebooks/Tweet sentiment/01_load_and_clean.ipynb

# Build intermediate; Report/ExAI_Report.pdf beside it is the deliverable.
Report/ExAI_Report_v2.html

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately TRACKED, for the avoidance of doubt:
#
#   src/, scripts/, config.yaml, requirements.txt, pyproject.toml
#   README.md, LICENSE
#   Report/ExAI_Report.pdf and Report/figures/*.svg
#   artifacts/summary_all.json, plausibility.json, faithfulness.json,
#     behaviour.json      — every figure and verdict in the report derives from
#                           these, so they are what makes it auditable
#   artifacts/<task>/summary.json, split.json, training_metrics.json
#   Notebooks/            — the superseded notebook version, kept as the record
#                           of what the rebuild replaced (three tweet notebooks
#                           excepted, see above)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The remaining notebook-era outputs: LIME and SHAP explanation pages for the
# movie task. These are not merely superseded -- they are the output of the
# defect this rebuild corrects. The original notebook loaded the LoRA folder
# with BertForSequenceClassification.from_pretrained, which silently ignores
# the adapter's fine-tuned head, so every one of these pages explains a
# randomly initialised classifier. models.py now asserts the head matches its
# checkpoint before any explanation runs.
#
# They stay on disk; publishing 60 MB of explanations of a broken model would
# be worse than useless. The report's corrections table records what happened
# and why, which is the part worth keeping.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
outputs/
